      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1407 and 903, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. This algorithm involves repeated division and taking remainders until we reach a remainder of 0. The last non-zero remainder is the GCD.\n\n1. Divide 1407 by 903:\n   \\[\n   1407 = 903 \\cdot 1 + 504\n   \\]\n   Here, the quotient is 1 and the remainder is 504.\n\n2. Next, we divide 903 by 504:\n   \\[\n   903 = 504 \\cdot 1 + 399\n   \\]\n   Here, the quotient is 1 and the remainder is 399.\n\n3. Now, we divide 504 by 399:\n   \\[\n   504 = 399 \\cdot 1 + 105\n   \\]\n   Here, the quotient is 1 and the remainder is 105.\n\n4. Next, we divide 399 by 105:\n   \\[\n   399 = 105 \\cdot 3 + 94\n   \\]\n   Here, the quotient is 3 and the remainder is 94.\n\n5. Now, we divide 105 by 94:\n   \\[\n   105 = 94 \\cdot 1 + 11\n   \\]\n   Here, the quotient is 1 and the remainder is 11.\n\n6. Next, we divide 94 by 11:\n   \\[\n   94 = 11 \\cdot 8 + 6\n   \\]\n   Here, the quotient is 8 and the remainder is 6.\n\n7. Now, we divide 11 by 6:\n   \\[\n   11 = 6 \\cdot 1 + 5\n   \\]\n   Here, the quotient is 1 and the remainder is 5.\n\n8. Next, we divide 6 by 5:\n   \\[\n   6 = 5 \\cdot 1 + 1\n   \\]\n   Here, the quotient is 1 and the remainder is 1.\n\n9. Finally, we divide 5 by 1:\n   \\[\n   5 = 1 \\cdot 5 + 0\n   \\]\n   Here, the quotient is 5 and the remainder is 0.\n\nSince the remainder is now 0, the last non-zero remainder is 1. Therefore, the greatest common divisor of 1407 and 903 is \\boxed{1}.",
