      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1407 and 903, we can use the Euclidean algorithm, which involves repeated division and taking remainders. The Euclidean algorithm states that the GCD of two numbers also divides their difference.\n\nLet's start with the numbers 1407 and 903.\n\n1. Divide 1407 by 903 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   1407 = 903 \\cdot 1 + 504\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 504. Now we need to find the GCD of 903 and 504.\n\n2. Divide 903 by 504 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   903 = 504 \\cdot 1 + 399\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 399. Now we need to find the GCD of 504 and 399.\n\n3. Divide 504 by 399 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   504 = 399 \\cdot 1 + 105\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 105. Now we need to find the GCD of 399 and 105.\n\n4. Divide 399 by 105 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   399 = 105 \\cdot 3 + 84\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 84. Now we need to find the GCD of 105 and 84.\n\n5. Divide 105 by 84 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   105 = 84 \\cdot 1 + 21\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 21. Now we need to find the GCD of 84 and 21.\n\n6. Divide 84 by 21 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   84 = 21 \\cdot 4 + 0\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 0. When we reach a remainder of 0, the divisor at this step is the GCD. Therefore, the GCD of 1407 and 903 is 21.\n\nThus, the greatest common divisor of 1407 and 903 is \\boxed{21}.",
