      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1407 and 903, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm is based on the principle that the GCD of two numbers also divides their difference. The algorithm proceeds as follows:\n\n1. Divide the larger number by the smaller number and find the remainder.\n2. Replace the larger number with the smaller number and the smaller number with the remainder.\n3. Repeat the process until the remainder is 0. The non-zero remainder just before the remainder becomes 0 is the GCD.\n\nLet's apply the Euclidean algorithm to 1407 and 903:\n\n1. Divide 1407 by 903:\n   \\[\n   1407 = 903 \\cdot 1 + 504\n   \\]\n   The remainder is 504.\n\n2. Now, divide 903 by 504:\n   \\[\n   903 = 504 \\cdot 1 + 399\n   \\]\n   The remainder is 399.\n\n3. Next, divide 504 by 399:\n   \\[\n   504 = 399 \\cdot 1 + 105\n   \\]\n   The remainder is 105.\n\n4. Now, divide 399 by 105:\n   \\[\n   399 = 105 \\cdot 3 + 94\n   \\]\n   The remainder is 94.\n\n5. Next, divide 105 by 94:\n   \\[\n   105 = 94 \\cdot 1 + 11\n   \\]\n   The remainder is 11.\n\n6. Now, divide 94 by 11:\n   \\[\n   94 = 11 \\cdot 8 + 6\n   \\]\n   The remainder is 6.\n\n7. Next, divide 11 by 6:\n   \\[\n   11 = 6 \\cdot 1 + 5\n   \\]\n   The remainder is 5.\n\n8. Now, divide 6 by 5:\n   \\[\n   6 = 5 \\cdot 1 + 1\n   \\]\n   The remainder is 1.\n\n9. Finally, divide 5 by 1:\n   \\[\n   5 = 1 \\cdot 5 + 0\n   \\]\n   The remainder is 0.\n\nSince the remainder is now 0, the GCD is the last non-zero remainder, which is 1. Therefore, the greatest common divisor of 1407 and 903 is \\boxed{3}.\n\nWe can verify this by using Python code. Let's implement this.\n```python\ndef gcd(a, b):\r\n    while b != 0:\r\n        a, b = b, a % b\r\n    return a\r\n\r\n# Calculate the GCD of 1407 and 903\r\ngcd_1407_903 = gcd(1407, 903)\r\nprint(gcd_1407_903)\n```",
